    "seq_model.json",
    "seq_model_full.json",        # RESEARCH (seq_model.py main_full + build_pretrain.py + tier2_seq_model.ipynb): the FULL Tier-2 architecture (the version the user specced, after the reduced 0.488 one). Shared conv encoder PRETRAINED on 100k genome-wide ENCODE cCREs to predict 311-TF ChIP binding FROM SEQUENCE (the dense aux task that solves data starvation), THEN fine-tuned with the sparse CRISPR head; compared WITH vs WITHOUT pretraining on Colab GPU, chromosome-held-out AUPRC + shuffle control. RESULT: pretraining SUCCEEDED (aux TF-binding-from-sequence AUC 0.77 -- the encoder genuinely learned the motif grammar) but did NOT transfer to regulation: pretrained fine-tune 0.479 ~ from-scratch 0.491 (within noise), both < epigenetics-only GBM 0.519 << TF-identity GBM 0.608; shuffle control 0.074 clean. FAIR COMPLETE NO-GO (nothing crippled this time) so the ceiling is REAL. WHY even done right: (1) cannot out-predict a measurement -- GBM gets 311 TFs binding MEASURED, encoder only PREDICTS it (AUC 0.77 lossy); (2) the fine-tune head already has the MEASURED activity tracks (DNase/H3K27ac/PolII/eRNA) as tabular features and sequence-predicted binding is largely redundant with those; (3) 569 positives cap the head regardless of encoder quality. Matches the field (CRISPR element-gene is dominated by measured activity+3D contact, not pure sequence). Architecture was sound; data reality (measured>>predicted) decides it. Production model = Tier-1 GBM (TF identity, 0.608). Both deep variants (reduced 0.488, full 0.479) honestly lose to the GBM on a fair test -- the deep-learning arc earned its no             # RESEARCH (seq_model.py + tier2_seq_model.ipynb): Tier-2 multi-task sequence CNN for CRISPR element->gene regulation, trained on Colab GPU. Conv motif encoder over 600bp element DNA -> AUX head predicts 311-TF binding from sequence (dense ~1.2M labels, learns the motif grammar) + regulation head on [embedding+8 tabular]. Chromosome-held-out AUPRC, 3 seeds + label-shuffle control. RESULT = honest NO-GO: seq CNN 0.488 (3-seed stable 0.486/0.483/0.496) BEATS distance (0.393, so it reads regulatory sequence better than proximity) but UNDERPERFORMS epigenetics-only GBM (0.519) and the TF-identity GBM (0.608). Label-shuffle control collapses to 0.081 (~base rate 0.055) = legit, no leak/overfit -- just not better. Two reasons: predicting binding FROM sequence is lossy (the GBM gets 311 TFs binding MEASURED), and 569 positives are too few for a from-scratch CNN to learn the combinatorial grammar. VERDICT: ship the Tier-1 GBM (TF identity, 0.608) as the production regulation predictor; the strategy discipline held (adopt deep only if it wins held-out -- it didnt). Revisit Tier-2 only with far more labelled regulation data OR a pretrained sequence foundation model (Enformer/Borzoi embeddings) instead of a scratch CNN. The honest ML deliverable: element->gene regulation at AUPRC ~0.61, SOTA-competitive + leakage-controlled + not over-engineered # RESEARCH (crispr_gate.py regate_compendium/identity_test): the decisive experiment the Tier-1 gate named -- rebuild the TF feature from the FULL ENCODE K562 TF-ChIP compendium (fetched all 311 TFs, 39x the prior 8) and re-gate (chromosome GroupKFold, AUPRC). TWO-PART RESULT. (1) COUNT ceilings: tf_count from 311 TFs (mean 49/element) does NOT grow the margin, it shrinks it (0.495->0.471; a dense count is a noisier active-region proxy than the sparse erythroid count). (2) IDENTITY passes: testing WHICH of 311 TFs bind (311 binary features, leakage-free = no label-based selection) lifts AUPRC to 0.608 (3-seed stable 0.601-0.612) vs 0.519 epigenetics-alone vs 0.393 distance -- and the LABEL-SHUFFLE CONTROL collapses to 0.069 (~base rate 0.055), proving no leak/overfit despite 311 features. So the combinatorial-TF hypothesis is CONFIRMED via identity (which TFs co-bind), not count (how many) -- the count discards the signal identity keeps. VERDICT = PASS/GO for deep learning: a tree over binary TF-occupancy already hits 0.608 (+0.09 over epigenetics, +0.21 over distance), so a sequence model learning TF motif grammar + combinations directly is JUSTIFIED. The gate worked across both rounds: stopped a premature deep-net spend on the wrong feature (count), then gave a clean controlled GO once the right feature (identity) was tested. Feature-level leak check clean (top TFs RNF2/GATA1/FOXM1/RUNX1 = real biology, r~0.25)            # RESEARCH (crispr_gate.py): Tier-1 Go/No-Go for the regulation ML model. Mechanistic K562 feature matrix (distance, element+promoter DNase/H3K27ac/PolII/eRNA signal, TF-ChIP count, gene expr, ABC) -> XGBoost, GroupKFold BY CHROMOSOME, AUPRC, vs distance-only + ABC-only baselines on the same folds (monotonic constraints + scale_pos_weight). 10,331 CRISPR element-gene pairs (ENCFF968BZL), 5.5% base rate. The distance trap is real (hits median 34kb vs non-hits 388kb -> distance-only AUPRC 0.405, the true bar; ABC-only 0.247 GLOBAL is abstention-deflated -- scores only ~8% of pairs, ~0.58 where it fires). Result: XGBoost(raw epi) 0.495 = +0.090 over distance, 4/5 folds. VERIFIED by a 4-lens adversarial workflow (ablation, distance-control, seed-robustness): the +0.09 is REAL orthogonal signal, not distance repackaged -- GBM beats distance WITHIN all 4 distance bins (+0.108/+0.063/+0.028/+0.006), robust across 3 seed reshuffles (+0.095/+0.109/+0.111), chromosome-disjoint folds (no leak). The orthogonal lift is almost entirely tf_count (drop-one -0.042; h3k27ac -0.013 second; epi-only 0.216 = 4x base), and tf_count uses only ~8 ChIP tracks not the ~300-TF ENCODE compendium -> +0.09 is a FLOOR. VERDICT = CONDITIONAL PASS: task is learnable (real verified orthogonal signal beyond distance) but modest (~0.50 AUPRC) and single-feature-dependent; the disciplined next step is NOT deep learning but rebuilding tf_count from the full TF compendium + a feature-level leak check, then re-gate -- a sequence CNN is justified only if that shows the margin grows. The gate did its job: stopped a premature deep-net spend and named the one decisive experiment
